WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.540
Did you notice the Ashford Twins popping up
in "Welcome to Raccoon City?"

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.820
What about the homage to video game voice
acting's most awkward sandwich joke?

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.380
Which of these Easter eggs did you catch in
the newest "Resident Evil" movie?

00:00:12.407 --> 00:00:16.359
The zombie infection that overtakes Raccoon
City doesn't suddenly transform its victims

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:21.050
into undead monstrosities; it slowly weakens
their brains and deteriorates their bodies

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.419
until they die and are reborn as shambling
flesh-eaters.

00:00:24.419 --> 00:00:28.810
The original "Resident Evil," released in
1996, features a collectable journal called

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.969
"Keeper's Diary" that details a character's
mental descent into undead madness.

00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:37.040
They initially complain about feeling itchy
all over before their speech pattern becomes

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.000
simple and barely legible.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.079
They describe killing and eating a person,
calling him "tasty."

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:46.070
Their final journal entry only contains two
words: "Itchy.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.070
Tasty."

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.610
In "Welcome to Raccoon City," Claire Redfield
is visiting her brother's house when all hell

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.659
breaks loose in the quiet mountain town.

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:57.179
She is confronted by a woman who writes, in
blood, the words "Itchy Tasty" on the window

00:00:57.179 --> 00:00:58.340
of the Redfield house.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.170
For fans of the game, the words bring back
fond memories of one of the most intimate

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.600
and disturbing scares from the original "Resident
Evil."

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.520
In "Welcome to Raccoon City," Claire Redfield
is introduced in the passenger seat of an

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.910
18-wheel truck, having hitched a ride into
Raccoon City.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:16.259
When a woman wanders into the street, the
truck driver is too slow to notice and runs

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:17.259
her down.

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:21.840
This sequence is a nearly shot-for-shot homage
to the scene from 2019's remake of "Resident

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.369
Evil 2," right down to the truck driver's
fondness for juicy hamburgers.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:29.460
After hitting the woman, the man gets out
of his truck to investigate, but the zombified

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.450
woman gets up and walks away from the accident
while he's not looking.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.530
In the movie, his dog licks up some of the
woman's blood, unwittingly infecting itself

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.720
with the T-Virus, at which point it bites
its owner.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.289
In the game, however, the truck driver has
no dog, and is instead bitten directly by

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:44.470
the undead woman.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.469
The first zombie ever shown in "Resident Evil"
remains one of the most memorable moments

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.439
in the history of the survival horror genre.

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:54.359
The iconic image of a flesh-eating zombie
taking a bite out of a S.T.A.R.S.

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:58.049
member before slowly turning its attention
to the player is seared into the brains of

00:01:58.049 --> 00:01:59.570
generations of video game enthusiasts.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:05.470
The sequence was recreated in the 2002 "Resident
Evil" remake, and now it's been re-recreated

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.520
for "Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City."

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.120
Agent HUNK made his video game debut in "Resident
Evil 2."

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.240
He briefly appeared in a cutscene when he
and his men confront William Birkin, but he

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.160
became notorious for his role in "The 4th
Survivor," an unlockable mode that follows

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.440
the gas mask-wearing operative as he attempts
to escape Raccoon City with limited ammo and

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.470
healing items.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.180
The notoriously difficult mode, featured in
both the original and 2019 remake, made HUNK

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.800
an instant fan favorite.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.240
but he's only made sporadic appearances since,
usually in non-canon modes like the Mercenaries,

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.820
and never without his signature face covering.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:41.890
As Umbrella puts the titular town under lockdown
in "Welcome to Raccoon City," a soldier is

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.410
shown in a costume nearly identical to the
one worn by HUNK.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.200
It's unlikely he's supposed to be "Mr. Death"
himself, but his gas mask and all-black ensemble

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.150
are clearly meant to evoke Raccoon City's
"4th Survivor."

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.700
Jill Valentine has a wicked sense of humor
in "Welcome to Raccoon City."

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.640
She's introduced drawing her sidearm, seemingly
willing to perform a William Tell-esque trick

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.380
shot on a sleeping Leon Kennedy.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.870
"Haha, that's actually hilarious."

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:12.780
Later on, she demonstrates her twisted sense
of humor yet again when she ponders the question,

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.910
"How would you rather die: being swallowed
whole by a giant snake, or eaten by a great

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:17.910
white shark?"

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:18.910
Her S.T.A.R.S.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.690
allies are disturbed by the thought, and for
good reason: In the original "Resident Evil,"

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:25.320
both of these ghastly fates can befall Richard
Aiken.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.390
In Jill's scenario, Richard can be devoured
by Yawn, a giant monster snake; in Chris'

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:36.230
scenario, he can be torn apart by a Neptune,
a great white shark infected with the T-Virus.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.840
"Welcome to Raccoon City" features the Raccoon
City Orphanage, where Chris and Claire grew

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:40.840
up.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.630
The orphanage, particularly its outer gates,
looks completely identical to the location

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:48.200
seen in the remakes of "Resident Evil 2" and
"Resident Evil 3."

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.370
In those games, like the movie, the orphanage
is a front for Umbrella's twisted experiments

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.870
on innocent children.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.620
Though the exterior is the same between game
and movie, the location plays a different

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:58.790
role in the games.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:03.091
In the "Resident Evil 2" remake, the location
is visited by young Sherry Birkin, who is

00:04:03.091 --> 00:04:05.620
chased by a more villainous Chief Brian Irons.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.070
Meanwhile, "Welcome to Raccoon City" has a
less villainous version of Irons bring Leon

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.920
and Claire to the orphanage since it contains
a secret passage to the Spencer Mansion.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:19.430
The orphanage isn't the only video game location
recreated for "Welcome to Raccoon City."

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.460
The original Spencer Mansion has certain rooms
that are reproduced with staggering detail,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.900
including the stairwell room with all the
paintings on the wall, and the piano room

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.730
and its connecting secret passage.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:33.330
A character even has to play "Moonlight Sonata"
on the piano in order to open up the secret

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.330
room.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:38.090
On the other side of town, the foyer of the
Raccoon Police Department HQ has been fully

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:39.090
recreated.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.090
The S.T.A.R.S.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:44.389
office is similarly reproduced, looking just
as it did in the original "Resident Evil 2,"

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:48.460
and the holding cells, while located in a
different part of the building from the original

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.660
game, look just like the original.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.240
The protagonists of the "Resident Evil" video
games would be dead a thousand times over

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.599
if not for their use of first aid spray.

00:04:56.599 --> 00:05:00.930
This restorative item, ironically manufactured
by the Umbrella Corporation, raises the player's

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:06.030
health to full and has been used in nearly
every "RE" game since the 1996 original.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:10.060
It's unclear exactly how it works, but it
seems Umbrella, despite their heinous track

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.629
record of evil misdeeds, actually does make
useful products that help save lives.

00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:17.960
Eagle-eyed viewers will notice a fleeting
glimpse of first aid spray in "Welcome to

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:18.960
Raccoon City."

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.711
While a physical can of the spray doesn't
make an appearance that we noticed, the first

00:05:22.711 --> 00:05:26.419
scene of the movie, set in the Raccoon City
Orphanage, features artwork depicting the

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:27.430
healing item.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.479
A PSA billboard advertises the product, and
the artwork used to represent the can looks

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:34.219
exactly like the artwork from the classic
games.

00:05:35.219 --> 00:05:38.770
The Licker first appears in "Resident Evil
2," and has made numerous appearances since

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.650
then, popping up in "Resident Evil 5" and
most of the games set during the Raccoon City

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:43.650
outbreak.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:47.980
It also appears in three of the six Paul W.S.
Anderson-directed "Resident Evil" movies—1,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:52.639
2, and 5, for the record—and is one of the
most instantly recognizable Resident Evil

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:53.639
creatures.

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:57.510
In "Welcome to Raccoon City," only a single
specimen is seen, but it leaves a memorable

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.510
impression.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:02.439
First, it stalks Leon by crawling across the
ceiling, evoking the monster's intro from

00:06:02.439 --> 00:06:03.900
"Resident Evil 2."

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.860
Then it sneaks up behind Chief Irons, lifting
him up and killing him by eviscerating his

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:08.860
face.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.740
When his body drops to the ground, it resembles
the police officer found in a hallway in the

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:16.849
remake of "Resident Evil 2," whose face is
similarly disfigured due to a Licker attack.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.159
Released in 2000, "Resident Evil: Code Veronica"
follows up on the events of "Resident Evil

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:25.650
2," with Claire Redfield still searching for
her lost brother, Chris.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:29.430
Her journey takes her from Paris to Rockfort
Island to Antarctica.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:33.300
Eventually Chris and Claire do battle with
Alfred and Alexia Ashford, psychotic child

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.430
prodigy twins who developed the T-Veronica
virus.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:39.949
Claire first learns about the Ashford twins
when she discovers a film reel showing them

00:06:39.949 --> 00:06:44.250
as creepy children, with Alfred plucking the
wings off of a dragonfly before throwing the

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.400
helpless bug into a pit of ants, where it
is devoured.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.750
This film reel is replicated, almost shot-for-shot,
in "Welcome to Raccoon City," with the twins

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.729
even identified specifically as the Ashfords.

00:06:54.729 --> 00:06:59.029
There is an edited-in shot of Dr. Birkin watching
the creepy kids, implying that they might

00:06:59.029 --> 00:07:02.120
be from Raccoon, and not one of Umbrella's
European branches.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:06.259
Whether the Ashfords' appearance is a mere
Easter egg or a hook for a prospective sequel

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.419
remains to be seen.

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:12.030
The original "Resident Evil" games are akin
to mazes, with locations packed with locked

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.150
doors and tricky puzzles to solve.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.490
To help players get around, pausing the game
would bring up the map screen, showing the

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:21.719
player's location, which doors were locked
or unlocked, and which rooms had been explored.

00:07:21.719 --> 00:07:25.809
While future games would focus more on the
action and less on the puzzle-solving, the

00:07:25.809 --> 00:07:30.580
map remained, allowing players to easily retrace
their steps and figure out where to go next.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.870
As part of his betrayal of his fellow S.T.A.R.S.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:36.830
members in "Welcome to Raccoon City," Wesker
is given a Palm Pilot portable device with

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.449
specific instructions and a map of the Spencer
Mansion.

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:44.270
The map's style looks identical to those found
in the early PlayStation "Resident Evil" titles,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.849
but in monochrome black and white.

00:07:46.849 --> 00:07:50.169
A big part of the classic "Resident Evil"
formula is figuring out how to unlock the

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:52.520
numerous doors in any given location.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.740
Sometimes, tasks need to be performed with
Key Items, which usually unlock keys to open

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:57.740
specific doors.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:02.309
In "Resident Evil 2," both the original and
the remake, four particular keys are coded

00:08:02.309 --> 00:08:05.789
to the four suits in a deck of cards: diamond,
spade, club, and heart.

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:10.400
"Welcome to Raccoon City" features the same
keys, which are used by Claire and Leon to

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.759
open the underground passage to reach the
Spencer Mansion from the Raccoon City Orphanage.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:18.729
The prop keys look identical to their video
game counterparts, right down to the colors.

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:23.970
The original "Resident Evil" was infamous
for its poor voice acting, with badly translated

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:28.330
lines delivered by actors who probably didn't
have enough time to properly prepare or research

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.660
their characters.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:33.460
There's no shortage of memeable dialogue that
continues to be shared on internet message

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:37.760
boards to this day, but perhaps the most famous
occurs after Jill is rescued from a room with

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:42.600
a collapsing ceiling; after saving Jill from
being crushed to death, Barry Burton remarks,

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.030
"That was too close.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:47.700
You were almost a Jill sandwich."

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.400
The line is retooled in the 2002 remake to

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.451
"That was close.

00:08:51.451 --> 00:08:56.140
A second later, you would have fit nicely
into a sandwich."

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.590
The replacement arguably lacks the punchy
humor of the original, but at least the remake

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.940
features a Barry who can say "Jill" right.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.180
"Welcome to Raccoon City" puts its own spin
on the classic meme when Jill snatches Wesker's

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:10.920
sandwich off his plate before he can take
a bite.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:11.930
"You snooze, you lose.

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:13.470
It's a Jill sandwich now."

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:17.910
The costumes in the "Resident Evil" video
games play a key role in defining the characters

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:18.910
who wear them.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:23.000
Especially back in the days of low-polygon
3D models, effort had to be made to keep the

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.610
characters distinct.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:28.570
That's why Wesker has his spiky blonde hair
and sunglasses, Chris has his sleeves rolled

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:31.850
up, Jill has her beret, and Barry has his
red vest.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:36.920
As the visuals became more advanced, the characters
were redesigned to be more realistic and fashionable.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:40.230
Several characters in "Welcome to Raccoon
City" dress in a manner that evokes their

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:41.670
classic costumes.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:46.101
Claire wears clothes that are nigh-identical
to her outfit from the "RE2" remake, and Chris

00:09:46.101 --> 00:09:49.650
is dressed just like he did in the original
game, with his sleeves rolled up to show off

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:51.020
his beefy arms.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.420
Likewise, Wesker wears an all-black ensemble,
and the post-credits scene gives him his character's

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:57.080
signature sunglasses.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:01.270
Said post-credits scene also features the
debut of Ada Wong, who wears the same trench

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:03.900
coat she sports in the "RE2" remake.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:08.030
Even Leon looks a little more like his classic
video game counterpart once Claire instructs

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:10.150
him to don a set of RPD body armor.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:12.690
